  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:22:46.065Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nSubstituted filing of income tax returns is allowed for individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer in the Philippines, provided that the income tax has been correctly withheld by the employer. In such cases, the certificate of withholding from the employer, duly stamped by the BIR, serves as the substituted filing of the income tax return.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe relevant provision is found in **Section 51-A of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC)**, which outlines the conditions for substituted filing of income tax returns.\n\n### Interpretation\nAccording to Section 51-A of the NIRC, individual taxpayers who receive purely compensation income from a single employer and whose income tax has been accurately withheld are exempt from filing an annual income tax return. The employer's certificate of withholding, once received and stamped by the BIR, is considered sufficient for compliance with the filing requirement.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis provision simplifies the tax compliance process for employees with straightforward income situations, reducing the administrative burden on both taxpayers and the BIR. It ensures that employees who have their taxes withheld correctly do not need to file additional paperwork, streamlining the overall tax reporting process.",
